+    <section class="journal" id="journal">
+      <h2>Roaster's Journal</h2>
+      <div class="journal-text" id="journal-text">
+        <p>Every bag we roast begins months before the beans ever reach our shop. In early spring, our head roaster
+          travels, or, when travel isn't possible, spends long evenings on video calls, with the small farms and
+          cooperatives we've worked with for years. We ask the same questions every time: How was the rainfall? When
+          did the cherries start to ripen? What did you change this season? Those conversations matter as much as any
+          cupping score, because they tell us how the coffee was grown and who was paid to grow it.</p>
+        <p>We buy directly from producers wherever we can, and through a small number of trusted importers when we
+          can't. Direct relationships let us pay well above the Fair Trade minimum, usually two to three times the
+          commodity price, and commit to buying the same lots year after year. That stability lets farmers invest in
+          better drying beds, shade trees and processing equipment instead of gambling on a volatile market.</p>
+        <p>When samples arrive, we roast them small and taste them blind. Each coffee is cupped at least three times
+          on different days, by different members of the team, before we place an order. We look for sweetness, clarity
+          and balance first; a coffee that is exciting but fades by the third sip rarely makes the cut. Roughly one in
+          eight samples earns a place on our menu.</p>
+        <p>Once we've committed to a lot, the beans travel by ship and truck to our roastery, where we test moisture and
+          density, then run a series of test roasts to find the right profile. We rest every batch for at least four
+          days before it goes into a bag, and we print the roast date on every one so you always know how fresh your
+          coffee is. Seasonal favorites rotate with the harvest, which is why the single-origin lineup changes through
+          the year.</p>
+        <p>Finally, we publish where each coffee came from: the farm, the altitude, the variety and the price we paid.
+          Transparency keeps us honest, and it lets you taste the story behind every cup.</p>
+      </div>
+      <button type="button" class="btn" id="summarize-btn">Summarize</button>
+      <div class="journal-summary" id="journal-summary" role="status" aria-live="polite" hidden></div>
+    </section>
   </main>
 
   <footer>
     <div class="footer-links">
